+    /// Sets the stateless reset key, so a restarted endpoint can reset the connections of
+    /// its previous instance.
+    ///
+    /// When an endpoint stops without closing its connections (a crash, a killed process,
+    /// an evicted host), its peers only notice once their idle timeout expires. A QUIC
+    /// [stateless reset] lets the next instance tell them within a round trip: it answers a
+    /// packet for a connection it does not know with a reset, which the peer accepts only
+    /// if it was made with the key of the endpoint that issued the connection ID.
+    ///
+    /// By default a fresh random key is generated on every [`Builder::bind`], so resets only
+    /// work within the lifetime of one instance. Supplying the same key on every bind of an
+    /// endpoint (with the same [`SecretKey`] and reachable at the same addresses) lets a new
+    /// instance reset the connections of an earlier one. The key also seeds the validation
+    /// of connection IDs, because an endpoint only answers with a reset for connection IDs
+    /// it could have issued itself.
+    ///
+    /// Keep the key secret: anyone who knows it can reset this endpoint's connections. Use
+    /// a distinct key per endpoint, e.g. derived from the endpoint's [`SecretKey`] with a
+    /// key derivation function under an application-specific context.
+    ///
+    /// [stateless reset]: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000#section-10.3
+    pub fn stateless_reset_key(mut self, key: [u8; 32]) -> Self {
+        self.stateless_reset_secret = Some(StatelessResetSecret::new(key));
+        self
+    }
+
     /// Specify the rustls cryptography to use for all TLS operations.
     ///
     /// This includes
